menu "DGX"

config DGX_ENABLE_SPI
    bool "Enable SPI transport backend"
    default n

config DGX_ENABLE_I2C
    bool "Enable I2C transport backend"
    default n

config DGX_ENABLE_P8
    bool "Enable 8-bit parallel transport backend"
    depends on SOC_LCD_I80_SUPPORTED
    default n

config DGX_ENABLE_DSI
    bool "Enable MIPI-DSI transport backend"
    depends on SOC_MIPI_DSI_SUPPORTED
    default n

config DGX_DSI_HARDWARE_COPY
    bool "Move and fill pixels of a DSI display by the hardware of the chip"
    depends on DGX_ENABLE_DSI && SOC_DMA2D_SUPPORTED
    default y
    help
        Large pieces go past the processor, several times as fast: a part
        of a 16-bit virtual screen put to the display and, with two frame
        buffers, what one of them lacks of the other by the 2D DMA; a
        rectangle filled with a color by the pixel processing accelerator
        (PPA), and an 8-bit screen whose table is two colors mixed of them
        by it, unless the call that sends the screen has DGX_LUT_EXACT. It
        costs 0.8 KB of RAM.

        The accelerator has a public interface. The 2D DMA is driven
        through a private interface of IDF, esp_private/dma2d.h, and one
        field of a register of the chip is set directly, for IDF 6.0.3 and
        later do not set it for 16-bit pixels. Should an IDF not build with
        it, or the 2D DMA and the accelerator be wanted for something else,
        turn this off: the processor does all of it then.

