    bool "Enable dual-head virtual compositor"
    depends on DGX_ENABLE_VSCREEN
    default n

config DGX_ENABLE_MORPH
    bool "Enable dot morphing framework (bit-matrix morphs, glow/sprite renderers)"
    select DGX_ENABLE_VSCREEN
    default n

config DGX_HW_FONT_IN_IRAM
    bool "Keep the code that lays out a handwritten text in IRAM"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32
    default n
    help
        The code of a program and the tables of a font are read from flash
        through one cache of two ways. Where a line of a font falls on a
        set that holds two lines of hot code already, every stroke waits
        for flash: the same text takes up to twice as long to go
        through and up to a third longer to draw, by where the linker has
        put the font, and that changes from build to build. Code in IRAM
        is not read through the cache.

        This takes 4.1 KB of IRAM for src/hw_font.c and the two functions
        of src/font.c that find a glyph. Going through the strokes of a
        text does not depend on the place of the font then, the drawing
        still does by half as much; see DGX_BEZIER_IN_IRAM.

        IRAM of an ESP32 is 128 KB and no part of the common heap, so it
        costs no heap. A program with Wi-Fi may have a few KB of it left:
        the linker says "IRAM0 segment data does not fit" when it is not
        enough. Measured on an ESP32 only, see docs/pen-en.md.

config DGX_BEZIER_IN_IRAM
    bool "Keep the code that draws Bezier curves by a pen in IRAM"
    depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32
    default n
    help
        The same for src/bezier.c, the hard pen and the soft one: 9.3 KB
        of IRAM. The time of drawing a handwritten text does not depend on
        the place of the font then, within 2%. With DGX_HW_FONT_IN_IRAM
        too, 13.3 KB in all, neither the drawing nor the going through
        does. See docs/pen-en.md.

endmenu